==Format==
[[File:Exposición “¿De qué color pinta el verde? - El Colegio Nacional - 02.jpg|thumb|An [[Adidas Telstar]] football used at the 1970 World Cup matches.]]
The 16 qualified teams were divided into four groups of four. Each group was played as a [[Round-robin tournament|single round-robin]] in which each team would play each of the others in their group once. Teams were ranked by the number of points earned from their matches (two points were awarded for a win and one for a draw), and goal difference was used as the primary tie-breaker; if two teams finished level on both points and goal difference, lots were drawn to separate them. This method was used to separate the top two teams in Group 1 (Soviet Union and Mexico),<ref>{{cite journal |title=Soviet Union wins toss in World Cup |journal=Miami News |date=1970-06-13 |page=2-B}}</ref> but it was not used to separate Bulgaria and Morocco in Group 4, despite them finishing level on points and goal difference, as they were both eliminated anyways.

The top two teams from each group progressed to the knockout stage. In all knockout matches, extra time was played if the teams were drawn after 90 minutes. If teams were still tied after extra-time, drawing of lots would have been used in all matches other than the final. FIFA did not announce in advance what would happen if the final itself were to be drawn after extra time.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Drawing of lots -- that's how teams will be parted |journal=Evening Standard |location=London |date=1970-05-26 |page=43}}</ref> In the event, all knockout matches were decided in either regular time or extra time.

==Final draw==
Although it was reported in the build-up to the final draw that seedings would be used, as had been the case at the previous two World Cup Finals,<ref>{{cite news |title=Ability and politics govern the draw |page=11 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=19 December 1969}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Top teams to be seeded |page=11 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=8 January 1970}}</ref><ref name="Seeding criteria negotiaions"/> the FIFA Organising Committee ultimately announced on the same day of the draw, that there would be no seeding of teams due to failed attempts to reach a compromise for the seeding criteria.<ref name="FIFA seeding">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/82/40/89/fs-201_12a_fwc-seeding.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120722050449/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/82/40/89/fs-201%5f12a%5ffwc-seeding.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 July 2012|publisher=FIFA |title=FIFA World Cup: Seeded teams 1930–2010 |date=November 2009}}</ref> Instead, the 16 qualified teams were divided into four pots comprising 'geographical groupings' decided by a vote of the FIFA Organising Committee, which for the composition of pot 1, 3 and 4 also took into account the teams' strengths and even political considerations.<ref name="Seeding criteria negotiaions">{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=9coVAAAAIBAJ&dq=stanley%20rous&pg=7074%2C2437127|work=[[Jornal do Brasil]]|language=pt|title=Brasil, Alemanha e Italia querem o Grupo Dois|date=10 January 1970|archive-date=1 May 2021|access-date=7 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210501112544/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=9coVAAAAIBAJ&dq=stanley+rous&pg=7074,2437127|url-status=live}}</ref> The tournament structure ensured that teams grouped within the same pot would not meet each other before the quarter-finals at the earliest. The system of politically decided pots hereby ensured that Israel and Morocco would not be drawn to face each other after Morocco had earlier threatened to withdraw from the tournament, as they had done from the Olympic football tournament two years earlier,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sports-reference.com/olympics/summer/1968/FTB/mens-football.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200418130309/https://www.sports-reference.com/olympics/summer/1968/FTB/mens-football.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=18 April 2020|publisher=Sports-reference.com|title=Football at the 1968 Ciudad de México Summer Games: Men's Football}}</ref> if that were the case.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.currybet.net/cbet_blog/2009/12/africa-in-the-fifa-world-cup-p-2.php |publisher=Currybet.net |title=Africa in the FIFA World Cup: Part 3 – The Seventies |date=2 December 2009 |access-date=23 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121124045531/http://www.currybet.net/cbet_blog/2009/12/africa-in-the-fifa-world-cup-p-2.php |archive-date=24 November 2012 |url-status=live}}</ref>

